  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a stretch of dry and seasonably mild temperatures through\nthe upcoming weekend, our attention turns to a coastal storm\ndeveloping off the Southeast U.S coast. Large scale ridging \npositioned over the eastern and central Atlantic will help \ndirect the coastal storm up the eastern seaboard Sunday into \nMonday and may help focus and extend a plume of tropical \nmoisture ahead of it. Upstream shortwaves over southern Canada \nand the Plains will help steer the coastal low and influence its\ntrack. There is good consensus among the individual ensemble \nmembers compared to their respective ensemble mean from the \nGEFS, ECMWF and GEPS for the coastal low to remain just far \nenough off the East Coast that the axis of heavy rainfall \nremains mainly south and east of the Capital District. The \nlatest cluster analysis from the LREF which includes the GEFS, \nENS and GEPS indicates that 80% of the members favor this \nsolution which is rather impressive given the event is still 3-4\ndays away. While there is increased confidence on the placement\nof the heavy axis, there is lower confidence in the exact \nrainfall amounts. The reason for this stems from the fact that \nguidance still suggests that as tropical moisture from the \ncoastal low extends poleward Sunday into Monday, the moisture \nencounters a region of increased forcing for ascent as it tracks\ninto the equatorward entrance region of an anti cyclonically \ncurved jet. This may result in a a predecessor rainfall event \n(PRE) event ahead of the main coastal low and a corridor of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall. Confidence in exactly how much rain \nfalls within this axis of heavy rain remains low to moderate but\nthe latest LREF shows a 30 to 60% chance of at least 1 inch of \nrain falling in the mid- Hudson Valley, central/southern \nTaconics, NW CT, and western MA in the 24hr period from 1PM \nSunday to 1PM Monday with only a 10 to 20% chance for over 2 \ninches. Rainfall amounts decrease heading north and west. Given \nthese trends and the recent stretch of dry weather, flooding \nappear unlikely. \n\nWe will continue to monitor trends as the exact storm track will\ndictate which areas receive the highest rainfall amounts and if\na PRE can develop. While the NHC shows a 60% chance of cyclone \nformation over the next 7 days as this system tracks up the \nSoutheast U.S coast, the overall heavy rain and flooding impacts\nwill likely remain the same.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing our coastal storm from Sunday into Monday, there is\nmedium to high confidence that a rather potent cold front \npushing through the Northeast Monday night will usher in much \ncooler temperatures Tuesday through the Thanksgiving holiday.\nGiven the continued active subtropical jet stream in our El \nNino pattern, there is increasing confidence in yet another\nstorm system developing in the Southeast U.S and tracking up the\nEast Coast around the Thanksgiving holiday. With the antecedent\ncool air mass and high pressure positioned in the Canadian\nMaritime/northern New England, we will need to monitor precipitation\ntype should the storm track allow precipitation to extend into \nthe Northeast. Latest cluster analysis from the LREF shows \nroughly 70% of the ensemble members favor troughing developing \nover the central and eastern CONUS with a 40 to 80% chance of \nat least 0.25\" of precipitation falling over much of eastern NY\nand western New England in the 24hr period from 1AM Thurs to \n1AM Fri. Given we are still a week away from the event, this \nremains a low confidence forecast but considering the medium to \nhigh confidence in cooling temperatures and signs of a storm \nsystem occur during this busy travel period, we wanted to start \nmessaging the storm potential.",
